TASKBARMATCH 1.1.2 BETA - AUTOMATIC TASKBAR COLOR AND CONTRAST

Stop the old version through its tray menu before launching this update.
Double-click TaskbarMatch-1.1.2.cmd to start. Run it again to stop and restore.
A previously enabled Start with Windows entry is updated to this runtime;
auto-start is never enabled unless it was already selected by the user.

Light taskbar backgrounds use dark taskbar glyphs/text and dark backgrounds
use light glyphs/text. Theme requests are now restricted to the foreground
controls that need contrast, plus the Start/Search controls. They are no longer
applied across the whole taskbar visual tree. Search, clock, date and system
glyphs remain readable without recoloring unrelated interactive surfaces.
Full-color bitmap application icons and colored badges are not recolored.
Global system/app themes, accent colors, and Start are not edited.
Hover states, task-thumbnail previews and the tray-overflow flyout are explicitly
excluded from TaskbarMatch theming so Windows renders them with its native surface.
A small luminance hysteresis prevents contrast chatter near the crossover.

The background is still sampled independently for each monitor from a narrow
3-pixel strip. Original window selection, multi-monitor scaling, full-screen
and auto-hide behavior, 40 ms event cap and 250 ms content polling remain.
These intervals are scheduling settings, not measured latency/CPU promises.
No screenshots are stored. No information is uploaded.

One launch file. The native x64 helper, its complete source, build recipe and
compiled C# runtime are cached in %LOCALAPPDATA%\TaskbarMatch.
This version requires no download and does not use ExplorerTAP.dll.
It uses one local XAML diagnostics helper for both background and contrast.
The DLL is hash-verified before it is loaded into Explorer. No admin request.

Target: stock modern x64 Windows 11 taskbar, build 22621 or later.
Do not run another taskbar styler alongside this utility. The helper changes
private Explorer XAML controls, so compatibility with every Windows build
is not guaranteed. This is a beta. Its native PE was cross-compiled and its
interface layouts checked; live Windows/Explorer operation was not tested
in the authoring environment. The C# runtime compiles and self-tests locally.

Pause/Exit restores saved local property values and brushes. The helper also
watches the controller process to restore after a crash. Its library remains
loaded until Explorer exits. No Explorer restart is forced by this utility.
Stop both versions before switching between Taskbar Match builds.

Diagnostics: tray menu -> Diagnostics. Logs: runtime.log and build.log in the
cache directory. The per-monitor status shows the chosen text/icon contrast.
To remove: disable Start with Windows, exit, delete the launch file and cache.
A loaded DLL may remain locked until the next sign-out.

